<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		Replace TEAMID and com.example.yourapp with your own values (Apple
		Developer account team id + your app's bundle id). Both must match
		the provisioning profile Xcode signs the build with.
	-->
	<key>application-identifier</key>
	<string>TEAMID.com.example.yourapp</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
	<string>TEAMID</string>

	<!--
		"development" talks to APNs' sandbox gateway (api.sandbox.push.apple.com);
		"production" talks to the live gateway. This must match the environment
		the relay's APNS_SANDBOX flag targets (see website docs) — a token
		registered against the wrong environment gets InvalidProviderToken /
		BadDeviceToken back from Apple and the device never wakes for sync.
		Xcode also derives this from the provisioning profile at archive time,
		so a mismatch here is usually caught at submission, not at runtime —
		but keep it explicit for local/TestFlight builds.
	-->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>

	<key>get-task-allow</key>
	<true/>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>TEAMID.com.example.yourapp</string>
	</array>

	<!--
		OPTIONAL — required only for the Notification Service Extension flow
		(rich alert-class banners rewritten by wavesync_nse_handle_push).

		The app and its NSE run as separate processes and can only share the
		database through an App Group container. Uncomment on BOTH targets
		(app + .appex) with the same group id, register the group id in the
		Apple Developer portal, and point WaveSyncDbBuilder at the container
		via wavesync_app_group_container(groupId). Apps without an NSE need
		none of this — silent background wakes work without an App Group.

	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.com.example.yourapp</string>
	</array>
	-->

	<!--
		Deliberately NOT requesting com.apple.developer.networking.multicast.

		That entitlement is required for a raw multicast/broadcast responder
		(which is what libp2p's mDNS discovery is) to work on a physical iOS
		device — it works in the Simulator without it, which is a trap: a
		build that "works" in Simulator testing can still fail silently on
		every real device. Apple grants it case-by-case on request, it is not
		self-service, and approval is not guaranteed.

		This template is the relay-only deployment: WAN sync goes through the
		circuit-relay + rendezvous server (see website docs, "Relay
		deployment"), which needs no local-network access at all. Only add
		this entitlement — after requesting and receiving it from Apple — if
		you also add NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription/NSBonjourServices below
		for same-Wi-Fi LAN discovery. Until then, mDNS discovery is inert on
		iOS regardless of what the Rust engine is configured to try.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
